<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=plough">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plough</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A plough is a farm tool for loosening or turning soil before sowing; ploughs were at first powered by people, and the larger ox-drawn ard, or scratch plough, followed the domestication of oxen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>, perhaps as early as the 6th millennium BCE. A ploughed field from c. 2800 BCE has been found at Kalibangan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> made the earliest known heavy iron mould-board ploughs, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the general adoption of the heavy plough seems to have accompanied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-field-system">three-field system</a> in the 8th–9th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plough.jpg" alt="Plough" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akkadian_seal_Agricultural_scene_Louvre_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Kalibangan, India</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Ox-drawn ards may go back to the 6th millennium BCE in Mesopotamia and the Indus Valley.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/domestication">Domestication of animals<span>by 13,000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/seed-drill">Seed drill<span>c. 1400 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/three-field-system">Three-field system<span>771–256 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/iron-plough">Iron plough<span>c. 500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/horse-collar">Horse collar<span>477–499 CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=plough">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
